{"id": 363296, "name": "Davis v. Fowler", "name_abbreviation": "Davis v. Fowler", "decision_date": "1882-05-12", "docket_number": "No. 1212", "first_page": "590", "last_page": "590", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "17 S.C. 590"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [], "analysis": {"cardinality": 95, "char_count": 799, "ocr_confidence": 0.421, "sha256": "543abec473c23f4ccd20849eb01d5c8b2ba6ef758fc6047b551c013aa1a01964", "simhash": "1:b8b623ee90229943", "word_count": 140}, "last_updated": "2023-07-14T20:37:44.647210+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["Davis v. Fowler,"], "opinions": [{"text": "This was a case in chancery involving the genuineness of the signature to a note, and if genuine, whether the signature was obtained by fraud, deceit or undue influence. The Circuit Judge, Wallace, held that it was a valid and binding obligation, and on appeal his decree was affirmed. The Court uses this, language : \u201c This Court is very unwilling to disturb the finding \u2022of the Circuit Judge where the question is as to how much reliance can be placed upon the integrity or intelligence of a witness who can be present in the Circuit Court and not in this.\u201d Opinion by\nMr. Justice Fraser\n(sitting in the stead of the Chief Justice),", "type": "majority", "author": "Mr. Justice Fraser"}], "attorneys": ["Polo d\u00e9 Carlisle,Evans, Pomar d\u00e9 Simpson, for appellants; J. S. R. Thomson, J. W. Eerguson, contra."], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "No. 1212.\nDavis v. Fowler,\nNovember Term, 1881.\nMay 12th, 1882.\nPolo d\u00e9 Carlisle,Evans, Pomar d\u00e9 Simpson, for appellants; J. S. R. Thomson, J. W. Eerguson, contra."}, "file_name": "0590-01", "first_page_order": 604, "last_page_order": 604}